186246901}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a9be37b-bcf0-588b-a92a-b9f738cc9d0d" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-113372" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934099/assets/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{4}{5}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934099/assets/ea9bd36b532b46d3edcc99497b77e75288499a4b02239f5d2416cff6a4259478.png)\n\n- Translate it by $3$ units up to get the graph of $y=\\frac{4}{5x}+3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934099/assets/e6798ba049ab7040eec51d2da525d3383816578423e11384ced1f3665a2e50fd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "308f3685-0a90-56f3-9653-a83cec4286d2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for Some Unknowns Given the Graph of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1829099-3a4f-5126-995e-787b93198674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066972} {:answer/id #uuid "c09d5d47-c1f1-5c6a-8799-9288e8a859b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066973} {:answer/id #uuid "d825f93b-531e-572d-a8a2-86a00b72e2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066974} {:answer/id #uuid "58954f99-f051-5018-968c-196ed6fb48b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066975} {:answer/id #uuid "6c847618-b218-5550-98c5-0efd4a2acbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c847618-b218-5550-98c5-0efd4a2acbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066976} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb2b0c0-d68a-5295-973f-1225d6e540d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066971}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a12bd3e-7b6c-59fd-a15d-349dd424b687" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-39047" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/455/Source/Images/q-39047.png)\n\nThe graph represents the function $y = \\frac{a}{x} + k$. What is the value of $a + k$?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the horizontal asymptote of the graph is $y=1.$ This means that $k=1$ and we can re-write our function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{a}{x}+1.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are given that the point $(1,2.5)$ lies on the graph. Substituting its coordinates into the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{a}{x}+1 \\\\ 2.5 & =\\frac{a}{1}+1 \\\\ 1.5 & =\\frac{a}{1} \\\\ a & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+k=1+1.5=2.5.$"}]}
